      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.372,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "INTRODUCTION TO TRIGONOMETRY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "115"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.149,
            0.548,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "Note that the position of sides change when you consider angle C in place of A (see Fig. 8.5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.213,
            0.548,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "You have studied the concept of ‘ratio’ in your earlier classes. We now define certain ratios involving the sides of a right triangle, and call them trigonometric ratios."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.296,
            0.548,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "**The trigonometric ratios** of the angle A in right triangle ABC (see Fig. 8.4) are defined as follows :"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.626,
            0.146,
            0.887,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.702,
                0.354,
                0.79,
                0.373
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.361,
            0.592,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{sine~of~\\angle A} = \\frac{\\mathrm{side~opposite~to~angle~A}}{\\mathrm{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{BC}}{\\mathrm{AC}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.417,
            0.612,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{cosine\\ of\\ \\angle A=\\frac{side\\ adjacent\\ to\\ angle\\ A}{hypotenuse}=\\frac{AB}{AC}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.471,
            0.619,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{tangent of} \\angle \\mathrm{A}=\\frac{\\mathrm{side opposite to angle}}{\\mathrm{side adjacent to angle}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{BC}}{\\mathrm{AB}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.524,
            0.782,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{cosecant of} \\angle \\mathrm{A}=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{sine of} \\angle \\mathrm{A}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{hypot} \\bar{\\mathrm{s}} \\mathrm{a} \\mathrm{s}}{\\mathrm{side} opposite to a�除 \\mathrm{A}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}}{\\mathrm{BC}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.574,
            0.782,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{sccant~of~ \\angle A} = \\frac{1}{\\mathrm{cosine~of~ \\angle A}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{hypotenuse}}{\\mathrm{side~adjacent~to~angle~A}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{AC}}{\\mathrm{AB}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.627,
            0.825,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "cotangent of $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\frac{1}{1   agent of   \\angle \\mathrm{A}} = \\frac{ side adjacent to angle   \\mathrm{A}}{ side opposite to angle   \\mathrm{A}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{AB}}{\\mathrm{BC}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.684,
            0.901,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "The ratios defined above are abbreviated as sin A, cos A, tan A, cosec A, sec A and cot A respectively. Note that the ratios **cosec A, sec A and cot A** are respectively, the reciprocals of the ratios sin A, cos A and tan A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.75,
            0.796,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": "Also, observe that tan $\\frac{BC}{AB} = \\frac{\\frac{BC}{AC}}{\\frac{AB}{AC}} = \\frac{\\sin A}{\\cos A}$ and cot $\\mathrm { A } = { \\frac { \\cos \\mathrm { A } } { \\sin \\mathrm { A } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.831,
            0.901,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "So, the **trigonometric ratios** of an acute angle in a right triangle express the relationship between the angle and the length of its sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.873,
            0.902,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "Why don’t you try to define the trigonometric ratios for angle C in the right triangle? (See Fig. 8.5)"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
